Problems Found

1. Parameterized logging secret leak (CRITICAL): logger.info("Authorization: %s", secret) and logger.info("Cookie: %s", secret) redacted the format string to "Authorization=REDACTED" but left the original secret in record.args, which Python's logging then tried to format into the (now malformed) string, causing TypeError: not all arguments converted during string formatting and leaking the secret via the logging error handler. This was the bug I fixed.
2. setup_logger(level="INFO") ignored the explicit level and always used settings config — a semantic bug.
3. Minor: test_logger_security.py and test_file_utils.py not reflected in README's folder diagram.

Fixes (all applied in this conversation, uncommitted)

1. logger.py: Rewrote SensitiveDataFilter.filter() to (a) detect secret-format strings BEFORE touching args, (b) redact args to 'REDACTED' when the format string indicates a secret, (c) preserve %s placeholders in the message when args are present, and (d) only redact inline secrets when no %s placeholders remain. Added _is_secret_key() for dict-style %(token)s. Added credential-URL pattern. Changed setup_logger(level=None) to respect explicit level or fall back to settings.
2. target_manager.py: Added credential rejection in set_target() (already present in committed version per report — verified in working tree). Consistent status normalization.
3. scope_manager.py: Improved hostname/port normalization.
4. file_utils.py: Ensured FileOperationError not double-wrapped.
5. Tests: Added 27 regression tests.

Final Status

Phase 01: COMPLETE. 79/79 tests passing. CLI verified working. All Definition of Done items satisfied (README §23).

What Was Learned

- Python's logging module formats msg % args in getMessage(), so mutating record.msg to remove %s while leaving record.args intact causes a TypeError.
- The filter must run on record.args BEFORE the message is formatted, and must keep the format string's %s placeholders intact when args are present.
- Scope enforcement must be fail-closed: no scope loaded → out of scope; malformed resource → out of scope.

What Depends on It

- All future phases depend on Phase 01's ScopeManager, TargetManager, logger, exceptions, settings, and data contracts.
- Future modules must call ScopeManager.enforce_scope() or is_in_scope() before any assessment action.

What Depends on Later Phases

- Nothing in Phase 01 blocks future phases; Phase 01 is designed to be consumed, not modified.

